<ToolLayout title="SaaS MRR / ARR / Churn Calculator" description="Calculate Monthly and Annual Recurring Revenue, plus churn rate, from your customer count and pricing.">
  <SaasMetricsCalculator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why these metrics matter</h2>
  <p>MRR and ARR are the core health metrics investors and founders track for subscription businesses. Churn rate — the percentage of customers lost each month — directly determines how much new revenue you need just to stay flat.</p>

  <h2>Common SaaS Metrics Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Confusing gross churn with net churn</strong> — net churn accounts for expansion revenue from existing customers, which can offset or even reverse gross churn.</li>
    <li><strong>Not distinguishing customer churn from revenue churn</strong>, which can tell very different stories if larger customers churn at different rates than smaller ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This SaaS Metrics Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Tracking recurring revenue health month over month</li>
    <li>Reporting MRR/ARR and churn to investors or a board</li>
    <li>Understanding how much new revenue is needed to offset churn and grow</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>SaaS Metrics FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the difference between MRR and ARR?</strong> MRR (Monthly Recurring Revenue) is your predictable revenue per month; ARR (Annual Recurring Revenue) is simply MRR × 12, used more for annual planning and investor reporting.</p>
  <p><strong>What counts as a "good" monthly churn rate?</strong> Under 5% monthly churn is commonly considered healthy for SMB-focused SaaS, while enterprise-focused products often target under 1%, since larger contracts amplify the revenue impact of each lost customer.</p>
</ToolLayout>
